Durarara!! AU
Chapter 23 – Part 3
Celty’s hand found his chest under the blanket, palm flat over the black shirt where the proof was impossible to hide.
Are you okay. Your heart is racing.
Curtis swallowed. Long black braids shifted against the cushion. The ceiling offered no cover.
“Yeah. I’m fine, Celty… I-it’s just different from last time.”
Last time she had waited until he was asleep. Last time the measuring had been hers alone in the dark. This time he was awake for every inch of it, and the soft landing had a pulse she could feel under her hand.
Celty left her palm there.
If I asked for a favor… would you find it selfish?
The private channel was quieter than the stain-night demands. Closer to the spare-room honesty. Closer to don’t become whatever.
Curtis did not need a long time with that one.
“No.”
Plain. Immediate. The same boy who had said proceed as long as you’re comfortable and I would never force you.
Celty’s smoke thinned against his shoulder. For a second she only listened to his heart try to calm down and fail. Then the favor came out carefully, as if she were still testing the limit that refused to appear.
Then hold me. Properly. Not the polite couch distance. Not the version where you keep one arm free for the book and the exit. A short pause. I keep choosing closer and then stopping short of asking. That feels more dishonest than the ask.
Her forehead pressed nearer the side of his neck, empty space above her collar resting against him the only way it could.
If that is too much after the month, after the photos, after a day that already took too much out of you — say so. I will take the answer. I am only asking for your arms. Not the rest of your life. Not a replacement for the house that lied. Just… this. For a while.
Under the covers, the favor sat between them: simple, heavy, and no longer disguised as a test.
Curtis got closer.
The relief that moved through Celty was immediate and physical — black smoke easing, the rigid line of her shoulders unlocking as he closed the last polite inch she had been leaving him as an exit. He did as instructed. Arm around her. The other finding the blanket and her back. Not the half-hold of a man ready to reach for a book or a cigarette. Proper. Present.
Not only because she asked.
Because he felt the same way.
The private channel opened from his side without the flustered edge from a minute ago, still rough, still honest.
Yeah. Me too.
He settled her against him under the covers, long black braids spilling across the cushion and over the empty space where her head was not, mismatched eyes finally off the ceiling and half-lidded in the low light. The racing heart did not stop. It only had somewhere to put the noise. Her bare shoulder under his palm. The black lace. The fact that she had asked for arms and not a replacement life, and that the ask matched the thing he had been holding back since she left the spare room.
Different from last time, he sent, quieter. Last time I was already out. This time I get to mean it.
Celty’s hand stayed on his chest a second longer, feeling the proof, then slid to his side as she fitted into the hold she had requested. The wiped desk across the ward did not vanish. A month ago did not vanish. Anri’s doorway and the locked phone full of Justice did not vanish. For a stretch of night, they simply had less room to stand in.
Thank you, she answered, and there was no PDA formality in it. This is the landing. This is all I needed it to be.
Curtis held her.
Not as whatever.
Not as a soft answer to someone else’s house.
As the same want, admitted without a speech, under a blanket in a flat neither of them had chosen, while Ikebukuro kept its lights on and the two of them finally stopped performing distance they no longer felt.
Under the blanket, Celty’s private voice came through quieter, almost testing the hold for a different kind of limit.
I bet you wish I had a head.
Curtis chuckled against the empty space above her collar, the sound low and real, less flustered than the heart-race and more settled for having her in his arms properly.
“To be only attracted to the physical.” A soft pft. “You miss out what the person can truly show you. I learned the hard way when you only see the physical.”
The California girl sat in the sentence without a name. The one who had been beautiful and cruel. The one who had taught him that a face could be a trap as easily as a promise. He did not unpack it. He left the lesson where it belonged: against a dullahan who kept offering him the absence like it was a dealbreaker he had simply failed to notice.
Celty went still in his arms.
Black smoke rose once, thin and unsteady. If she had a face, the expression would have been difficult to name — not pure relief, not pure doubt. Something caught between being seen and being afraid the seeing was a performance.
You say that while you are holding me in lace and talking about beautiful skin.
A shorter beat, less accusation than wonder.
Most people flinch. Or they turn it into a fetish. Or they tell me I am fine the way I am in a voice that means they have already decided I should stop looking for what I lost. Her hand pressed lightly against his side. You are the first person who has called the missing part a reason you might see more of me, not less. That is either very kind… or very dangerous for me to believe.
She did not pull away.
If you learned it the hard way, then I will not argue with the lesson tonight. I will only say this: I still want my head. That does not mean I need you to wish I already had it in order to stay under this blanket.
A thinner, almost vulnerable line:
Thank you. For not making the empty space the first thing you try to fix.
Curtis’s arm stayed firm around her under the blanket, the words coming out without the fluster this time — plain, like the soft landing when it stopped being a theory.
“I will do everything in my power to help you find it.” A short pause. “And if you choose to return to your duties… I will be so happy for you.”
Celty went rigid, then slowly not.
Black smoke spilled in an uneven ribbon across the cushion. The private channel opened on silence first, then on something rawer than the stain photos, rawer than a month ago, rawer than asking to be held properly.
You would help me look.
And if I found it — if I became what I was before this city, before Shinra, before a foreigner who can hear me — you would be happy for me.
She pressed closer despite herself, forehead against his collarbone, the empty space above her collar tucked under his jaw as if the absence needed somewhere to hide while she thought.
Shinra wants me to stay as I am. He says it is love. Sometimes it feels like a cage with kind wallpaper. A thinner beat. You are offering to help me leave the version of me you are holding right now. That is… not what people say when they have me under their blanket.
Her gloved-not-gloved hand — bare against his shirt — tightened in the fabric.
I want my head. I have always wanted it. If you mean the offer, then you are promising to walk toward a future where I might not need this city, or this flat, or a soft landing in the same way. She did not pull back. That either makes you the most honest person who has ever touched me… or a man who has not finished imagining what ‘happy for you’ would cost him.
A last, quieter line, almost a plea not to dress it up:
Do not say it if it is only comfort. Say it if you would still help me search when finding it might take me away from you.
Under the covers, the favor of arms had become something larger: a dullahan asking whether the boy who could hear her would still choose her freedom over her presence, and wait for an answer that could not be half-true.
Curtis did not dress it up.
His arms stayed around her under the blanket, long black braids spilling over the cushion, mismatched eyes open in the dark like the answer had already cost him something and he was paying it anyway.
“I have lost so much.” A short, rough breath. “If I lose someone because they find what they are looking for and are happy… then it’s worth it.”
Celty’s smoke went completely still.
For a second the private channel was empty — no typing-in-the-mind, no sharp edge, just the weight of a boy who had said a month ago in a kitchen and was now offering to help her walk toward a future that might not include him, and calling that loss acceptable if it came with her joy.
You cannot keep saying things like that to me.
It was not a rejection. It was the sound of a defense failing.
She pressed her forehead harder against his collarbone, the empty space above her collar fitting under his jaw, hand fisted in his shirt like the sentence needed pinning to a body so it would not float away into something she could later pretend she had imagined.
I have a fortune that says a lost object will be found. I have been carrying it like a promise about my head. You are the first person who has made the finding sound like something that should still be allowed even if it takes me out of your arms. A thinner, shaken beat. Shinra would not say this. He would say stay. He would say you are enough without it. You are saying go — and that you will help me go — and that if I am happy, the empty space I leave behind is a price you will pay because you have already paid worse ones.
Her grip did not loosen.
That is either going to save me or ruin me for anyone who tries to love me with a cage.
A last line, quieter, almost against his skin:
I am still here tonight. I am still asking for your arms. But I am going to remember that you said the search matters more than keeping me. Do not take it back in the morning when the desk is still clean and the month is still a month. I will hold you to worth it.
Under the covers, the soft landing and the lost object occupied the same dark, and Celty held on to both — the boy who would help her find her head, and the promise that her happiness would be allowed to cost him, because he had already learned how to lose.
Celty held the promise against his chest and did not put the next thought on the channel.
That one stayed hers.
If I get my head back… you’re coming with me.
Not a negotiation. Not a soft landing. A quiet, stubborn counter-sentence to worth it. He could say he would be happy for her if the search took her away. He could mean it — she believed he meant it, and that was why the thought had teeth. She was not required to accept the loss he was already willing to underwrite.
Black smoke curled once under the blanket, private and fierce.
You have lost so much. That does not mean I get to be another clean exit you survive. If the lost object is found, I am not using the finding as a door that only swings one way. You help me search. You walk toward my freedom. And when I have a face again — when I have duties, a country, a past that is not only this city — you do not get to stand on the dock and wave like the month and the desk and this couch were just weather I passed through.
Her hand stayed fisted in his shirt.
Shinra wants me frozen.
You want me free.
I want you in the free version.
She did not say it.
Saying it would turn his worth it into an argument he would try to win on her behalf. Keeping it meant she could still take his arms for the night, still believe the offer to help her search, and still plan, in the place where only a headless woman kept her secrets, on a future where finding what she was looking for did not require leaving the boy who had called that loss acceptable.
If I get my head back, you’re coming with me.
Under the covers, she pressed closer and let him believe the silence was only gratitude.
Part of it was.
The rest was a claim she was not ready to make out loud — and not willing to surrender either.
Celty’s private voice came through against his chest, the fortunes suddenly back in the dark with them.
Your fortune cookie. I told you what it said. Did it mean me?
What you leave behind will follow you anyway.
Curtis was quiet for a second under the blanket, arms still around her, long black braids spilled across the cushion. The tourist-spot rant about Anri, Celty, or Rio sat behind his eyes — and then the simpler pattern that had actually been true since the open door and the tower and whether you’re sleeping or not.
“Yes.” The word was plain. “So far, when I think about it. Every time I run… you give chase.” A short, rough breath. “I can’t think of anyone except one person who has done that.”
Celty did not ask for the name. She felt it in the month, in the USB, in the way a month ago still made his fists try to close. Justice. The brother who had followed him through forests and borders and bad nights until following was no longer possible. And now a dullahan who would not let a drowned phone or a get out or a clean desk across the ward be the end of the sentence.
So I am the living half of the cookie.
Not a boast. A placement.
You leave. I follow. You hoped it was vague. It was not vague about me. Her hand pressed once against his side. I took that slip before Mei could. I kept it. I did not know I was keeping a description of myself.
A thinner beat, almost dry.
Good. Then you can stop being surprised when I show up. The cookie already told you I would.
She settled deeper into the hold he had given her, the lost-object fortune and the following-you fortune sharing the same dark, while Curtis held the one person left who still gave chase — and did not, for once, try to outrun the accuracy of a cheap piece of paper from Russia Sushi.
Curtis huffed a quiet breath into her hairline — or the place where a hairline would have been.
“I take it for what it is. A cookie.” A short pause, arm still firm around her. “Although… it was a little too accurate. However, I’m glad it’s right.”
Glad she gave chase.
Glad the following was not only a dead brother’s ghost.
Glad the cheap paper from Russia Sushi had named something he was done pretending was random.
Celty heard the gladness and did not leave it alone.
She pushed the boundaries again.
Not a leap. A slower test under the blanket: her leg sliding more fully over his, bare skin against fabric, her hand moving from his side to the front of his shirt, palm spread over the heart that had been racing earlier. Then closer still, the line of her body aligning until there was almost no polite gap left to retreat into. Black smoke held low, attentive, waiting for the flinch — in him or in herself.
It did not come.
No uncomfortable arrived for her.
None showed in him either. Only the same steady acceptance that had turned a page while her hand climbed his leg, that had said proceed, that had held her properly because he felt the same way. The absence of alarm was becoming its own kind of answer.
Still nothing, she thought, almost unsettled by how clean it was. I keep waiting for the night to tell me I have taken too much. It keeps not saying it.
The private channel opened, quieter than the test.
You are still comfortable.
Not a question this time. A verification against his body, his breath, the arms that had not loosened.
Curtis’s reply came without the old panic in it.
Yeah. Still comfortable. You?
Celty pressed closer instead of answering in words first — the last small boundary she could find without turning the hold into something that needed a new name — and found only the same missing discomfort, the same soft landing that refused to become a cage or a crime.
Still, she sent back. I do not know what it means that I can keep going and the uncomfortable never shows. I only know I am not ready to stop on principle alone.
Under the covers, the cookie stayed accurate.
Celty kept choosing closer.
And the limit she kept looking for still declined to arrive.
Curtis’s arms tightened a fraction — not to stop her, not to pull her in harder, just to put a voice in the way of the next test before it outran the night.
“Don’t push nothing you will regret in the morning.”
The line came out low against the empty space above her collar, careful with the month, careful with the wiped desk, careful with a dullahan who kept finding no uncomfortable and kept going anyway. Soft landing. Not a free fall with no way to name it at sunrise.
Celty went still against him.
Then the private channel answered, quieter than the boundary-pushing, more exposed than proceed.
That’s what somewhat scares me. A short beat. I don’t think I will.
Black smoke curled once under the blanket, less weapon than admission.
I keep waiting for the regret to show up with the uncomfortable. Neither of them is here. That means either this is right… or I am past the point where principle can save me from wanting it. Her hand stayed on his chest. Morning will still have Shinra’s kitchen. Mei’s laugh. A clean desk. Your school. The month. I know what the room will contain when the light comes back. I still do not feel the regret from here.
A thinner line, almost a warning to herself as much as to him:
If you are asking me to stop so I will not hate myself later, I hear you. If you are asking me to stop because you need the morning to be simple… say that. Do not make my missing regret into your responsibility unless it is actually about you.
She did not pull away.
She only held the truth between them: a woman who could not find the limit, a boy cautioning her about sunrise, and a night that was still refusing to become a mistake no matter how close she chose to sit inside it.
Curtis’s eyes looked away.
The ceiling. The dark. Anywhere that was not the empty space above her collar while the honest part finished climbing out of him. Long black braids shifted against the cushion. His arms did not loosen.
“…That’s the thing, Celty.” A rough little swallow. “I don’t want you to stop…”
It landed in the quiet like a second fortune, less cheap and more dangerous. The caution about morning regret had not been a polite exit. It had been a man trying to give her a brake he was not sure he still had for himself.
Celty’s smoke stuttered.
The private channel opened on a held breath.
…Oh.
Not triumphant. Not teasing. The sound of someone who had been testing for a wall and found, instead, that the other person was also past the line where principle did all the work.
So the morning warning was not you needing me to leave the covers. Her hand pressed once over his heart, feeling the race return. It was you wanting me to stay in them, and trying to make sure I still could walk out without blaming you.
She did not surge forward to punish the admission. She only turned his face back toward her with careful fingers at his jaw, empty space above her collar close enough that the absence felt like a forehead against his.
Then we are the same kind of in trouble. A thinner beat. I do not want to stop. You do not want me to stop. And both of us are still trying to leave the other person an exit.
Her voice dropped lower in the link.
Look at me. Not the ceiling. If we keep going, we keep going knowing the morning will be complicated. No pretending it was only a soft landing and a cookie. Can you do that… or do you need me to be the one who stops for both of us?
Under the covers, the question waited: not whether the uncomfortable would arrive, but whether two people who both wanted the next inch could admit the cost of it before sunrise and still choose it anyway.
Eventually they crossed that line.
It was not a sudden scramble. It was the last inch of a night that had already refused every stop sign: the spare room abandoned, the hand on his leg, the mask cracked, I don’t want you to stop, I don’t think I will regret it. Clothes came off in pieces between held breaths and private-channel checks — still comfortable? / still — until there was only skin against the beautiful skin he had named, and the soft landing became the whole of the couch, the blanket, the dark.
Celty got more than she bargained for.
She had known, in theory. Two nights ago her hand had found the truth of him in the dark while he slept, and holy shit had been a private fact she carried into this evening. Knowing and taking were different countries. When she finally had him without fabric between them, the size of what she had only brushed before made her whole body go taut, black smoke spiking once before she forced it down. She adjusted. He waited. The rule still held: only while she wanted it. When she took him in, the stretch pulled a reaction out of her that had nowhere public to go.
Without a head, her moans and screams were silent.
Curtis still heard her.
Every fractured sound arrived on the private channel like a live wire — raw, unfiltered, the voice only he in the city could receive — ah— Curtis— wait, no, don’t stop— layered over the silent arch of her back and the grip of her hands on his shoulders. He answered the heard and the seen at once, pace shaped to the way her smoke shuddered, to the way her thighs locked, to the wordless breaks where she simply flooded the link with feeling instead of language.
She loved that he did not fall for her over a fetish like Shinra.
Shinra had loved the dullahan, the absence, the myth of her. Curtis had called her skin beautiful, had offered to help her find her head, had said he would be happy if she left and was whole — and still held her like a person, not a specimen, not a headless miracle to be collected. When she came apart under him, silent to the room and deafening on the channel only he could hear, it was not because he was fucking the missing part of her. It was because he was there with what remained, and what remained was enough for him to stay inside the night without needing her completed.
After, the blanket found them again.
Celty’s smoke lay thin and spent across his chest. Curtis’s heart hammered under her palm the way it had when she first noticed it racing. The wiped desk across the ward did not exist for a few minutes. Neither did the month. Neither did the cookie.
Only the two of them, the line crossed, and a soft landing that had finally stopped being a metaphor.
Still here, she sent, rough and quiet.
Yeah, he answered. Still here.
Morning would be complicated.
They had already agreed to that.
For now the dark was allowed to keep what they had put in it.
Meanwhile, across the ward, Mei and Shinra were doing the same thing.
Albeit a little more explicit, and less passionate, than Curtis and Celty.
There was no soft landing in it. No private channel checking still comfortable. No fortune cookies or months or offers to help someone leave and still be glad. Shinra had her bent over the desk that had been wiped clean hours earlier, glasses still on, talking too much between thrusts about how he’d missed this exact angle, how Celty’s courier job was convenient, how Mei always knew when the apartment needed this kind of noise in it. Mei took him with practiced ease, one hand braced on the freshly polished wood, the other in his hair when she wanted him deeper, not because the night had become a vow but because the itch was mutual and the house was empty of a headless witness.
“Harder,” she said, flat and clear, and he obeyed like a man following a lab protocol he enjoyed.
Clothes were an afterthought on the office chair. The floor picked up new marks where the old ones had been erased. Mei came with a sharp, controlled sound and a laugh under it; Shinra followed soon after, forehead against her shoulder, already narrating how good she felt, how lucky he was, how this didn’t change the life he had with Celty — the same speech dressed as intimacy. Mei let him say it. She had never needed the speech to be true. She only needed the desk, the hour, and a partner who could not smell a lie the way a certain foreigner could.
When it was done, she reached for a tissue with the same practical motion she had used on the stains earlier.
“You should be more careful next time,” she murmured, not unkindly. “If she’d come home mid-wipe, your whole destiny speech would’ve had an audience.”
Shinra only smiled, flushed and bright and already rewriting the evening into something that still fit his map of love.
Across the city, on a couch that had not been cleaned of anything true, Celty and Curtis held a quieter aftermath.
In the office, Mei and Shinra put their clothes back on over evidence they would have to erase again before morning.
Same act.
Different night.
One of them had meant every inch of it.
The other had simply known where the furniture was.
After, the bed held them in a different kind of quiet.
Curtis’s heart had slowed under Celty’s palm. The blanket was a wreck. Long black braids stuck to his shoulder where her smoke had thinned into lazy ribbons. He did not launch into a speech. He did not name destiny. He only kept one arm around her, thumb tracing absent circles against bare skin, as if the soft landing still applied after the line was crossed.
Celty’s own thoughts were somewhat a mess.
Shinra had never made her feel that way during sex.
The comparison arrived uninvited and refused to leave. Years of a man who loved her with certainty, who narrated her body like a miracle he owned, who could make the act affectionate and frequent and still somehow about his devotion more than her nerves. She had not been unhappy in the simple physical sense. She had been… handled. Cherished as a dullahan. Taken as proof that he alone accepted the missing part.
Curtis had not been accepting a dullahan.
He had been with her — the voice only he could hear, the silent moans that still reached him, the woman who pushed limits and found no wall, the person he had offered to help leave if leaving made her whole. When she came apart it had not felt like being a specimen that finally rewarded patience. It had felt like being met. Size and all, history and all, the month and the desk and the cookie be damned.
That is inconvenient, she thought, almost grim against his chest. I cannot unfeel that. I cannot go home to a clean office chair and pretend the standard did not just move.
Black smoke curled once, private and agitated.
Shinra loves me. I have believed that. I still believe he believes it.
He has never left me shaking because he heard a sound I cannot make in the air.
He has never told me he would be happy if I found my head and walked away.
He has never made the absence feel irrelevant without asking me to stop wanting it filled.
She pressed closer under the blanket, not for round two, not for a claim she was ready to speak, only because the mess in her head got louder when there was space between them.
I asked for arms. I got a night that is going to follow me into every bed I have already agreed to occupy.
The cookie was right about me.
I am not sure I am ready for what that makes the morning.
Curtis’s private voice came through low, half-asleep around the edges.
You still here?
Still here, she answered, and kept the rest — the Shinra comparison, the raised standard, the fear that worth it and you’re coming with me had just become the same future — locked behind a mouth she did not have.
The soft landing, it turned out, could wreck a person more thoroughly than a desk ever had.
Justice was having the time of his afterlife.
Somewhere past the cheap seats, past the locked phone and the USB and the month-old silence, the dead brother was doubled over laughing hard enough that it would have knocked a drink out of his hand if he still had one.
There he is. There he fuckin’ is.
Curtis, under the blanket with a headless woman in his arms, could not hear a word of it. That only made Justice meaner and happier.
You absolute disaster. Soft landing. Spare room. “Don’t push nothing you’ll regret.” And then you fold like laundry the second she says she doesn’t want to stop. I told you. I told you on the roof. Do it. All night long. And you still tried to be a gentleman about it first. Iconic.
A wheezing pause, then the grin that had always shown up when Curtis’s careful plans met a real body.
Dullahan. On your couch. Heard every silent sound she can’t make in the air. And you’re lying there like a man who just discovered feelings have a physical setting. Bro. The suspenders outfit was already a confession. This is a whole thesis.
Justice circled the room he could not touch, delighted, unhelpful, fiercely proud in the only language he had ever used for it — mockery with the safety off.
Anri got “I like you a lot.” Celty got the soft landing and the whole night. You’re going to die of your own roster before the Undead ever find this country. I’m so proud. I’m so disappointed. I’m both. Bahahahaha.
On the couch, Curtis’s thumb kept its absent circle on Celty’s skin.
Celty kept her mess of thoughts to herself.
Neither of them heard the standing ovation from a boy who had been dead a month and was still, somehow, the loudest person in the flat.
Justice wiped a ghost tear that did not exist.
Sleep, you softy. You earned the complicated morning. I’ll be here when you panic about it. Obviously.
Celty did not realize she had won first place in the race gunning for Curtis.
She was not even aware there had been a race.
Across the same stretch of days, Rio had walked out of a hallway saying nobody was there for him and that she might go see him tonight. Anri had heard it, clenched a fist around a Nike box, muttered stay away from him, then claimed the later hour and left with a confession still warm in her hands. Both of them had moved toward his door with the sharp urgency of people who could see other women in the frame. Jealousy had been part of the fuel. Not the only part — care was real in both of them — but it had flavored the speed, the timing, the need to be the one who got there first.
Celty had not been racing.
She had photographed a couch. She had watched a desk erase itself. She had come through an open door rude and apologizing, asked for evidence, asked for arms, asked whether a soft landing could exist without becoming a theft. She had pushed limits because the uncomfortable would not arrive, not because Rio’s name was on a screen or Anri’s tea cup was in a sink. The second cup had registered. She had chosen not to interrogate it. That choice alone put her outside the contest the other two were running.
What made the difference was that.
Rio and Anri were both gunning for a place in him, and jealousy had made the target visible.
Celty had been gunning for the truth of her own house, for a voice that could hear her, for a night that did not demand she stay unfinished to be loved — and had somehow ended up under his blanket with first place in a race she never entered.
Curtis, half-asleep with her against his chest, did not know he had been a finish line.
Anri, a few blocks away, did not know the later hour had already been overtaken by a dullahan and a wiped desk.
Rio did not know the text about showing up for everyone else had landed on a night that was no longer empty.
Only the shape of it was clear from above, where even Justice might have stopped laughing long enough to whistle.
Celty had not taken him from anyone on purpose.
She had only refused to go home to a lie — and found, in the refusal, the place both of the others had been trying to reach first.
In the dark, she still thought the mess in her head was only about Shinra, and standards, and a cookie that had been too accurate.
She did not know she was also the person who had arrived without racing…
and still gotten there first.
Celty lay against his chest and did not put the next words on the channel.
They would have reached him if she had. That was the whole problem. He would have heard them the way he heard her silent moans, the way he heard are you okay, the way he heard every private thing the rest of the city never would. So she kept this one behind the absence of a mouth, in the place only she still owned.
I love you, Curtis.
The sentence arrived fully formed and terrifyingly calm.
Not the way Shinra said it — frequent, certain, attached to destiny and the shape of her soul. Not a claim staked in jealousy like the race she had not known she was winning. Just the quiet, disastrous fact of a dullahan who had come for a sample and a soft landing and somehow ended up loving the boy who would help her leave if leaving made her whole.
Black smoke stayed perfectly still so it would not give her away.
I love you, and you cannot hear this.
I love you, and the desk is still clean.
I love you, and a month ago your brother died.
I love you, and I am not telling you, because if I put it on the channel you will hold it like another fortune and I am not ready to watch what that does to the morning.
Her hand rested over his heart, feeling the steady beat she had raced earlier, and she let the three words exist only once, only for herself, pressed into the dark like a second claim beside if I get my head back, you’re coming with me.
Curtis shifted in half-sleep and tightened his arm around her without waking.
Celty closed the thought before it could leak.
I love you, Curtis.
Then she buried it under the soft landing, the cookie, the silent aftermath, and the lie of ordinary breathing, so that the first person in the city who could truly hear her would not hear the one thing she was not ready to have answered.
